EXT. DUST-CHOKED TRADING POST, HIGH PLAINS - AFTERNOON

A sagging porch. A horse trough green with scum. Wind pushes tumbleweeds past hitching posts that have not held a horse in weeks. A hand-painted sign reads: SALT - NAILS - SOULS.

ELIJAH "SPARROW" CROWFOOT (30s, braided hair, buckskin vest) leans on a porch rail, whittling. Inside the doorway sits POSTMISTRESS CLEMENTINE VANDERMEER (60s, gingham, shotgun across her lap) with a ledger. A rider approaches: SHERIFF AUGUSTINE BELL (40s, moustache, sweat-stained hat), trailed by a young deputy, WILLET FAIRBANKS (18, gun belt too big for his hips).

Bell dismounts and strides to the porch. Willet slides off his horse, trips over a stirrup, rights himself, and follows, one hand on his holster.

BELL
Sparrow.

SPARROW
Sheriff.

BELL
You were seen near the Henderson place.

SPARROW
Lot of people were near the Henderson place.

CLEMENTINE
(from the doorway)
Gus, that boy was at my counter all morning.

BELL
Was he?

CLEMENTINE
Bought nails.

BELL
How many?

CLEMENTINE
Enough for a coffin.

A cold quiet. Sparrow continues to whittle. Willet licks his lips.

WILLET
Sheriff, should I--

BELL
No.

Sparrow finishes the carving and holds it up: a tiny wooden bird. He sets it on the rail between them.

SPARROW
I didn't kill him.

BELL
No one said you did.

SPARROW
Then why's the boy sweating?

Everyone looks at Willet. He blanches.

WILLET
It's hot.

CLEMENTINE
^It's ninety degrees.

BELL
^It's ninety degrees.

They speak together, and Bell shoots her a look. She shrugs, rocks the shotgun on her lap, and says nothing more.

Sparrow slides the bird toward Willet. The boy stares at it, shaking, and as Bell turns to him, the wind drops. Even the tumbleweeds stop rolling.
